   "source": [
    "# 01 - Safety-critical systems, reliability and risk\n",
    "\n",
    "**Week 1; suggested duration: two teaching periods**\n",
    "\n",
    "## Learning objectives\n",
    "\n",
    "- Distinguish hazards, risks, faults, errors and failures\n",
    "- Calculate reliability and availability\n",
    "- Interpret series and parallel system structures\n",
    "- Construct the initial TK-101 hazard chain\n",
       ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "d6b5915c4605",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "import sys\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "ROOT = Path.cwd()\n",
    "if not (ROOT / \"data\").exists():\n",
    "    ROOT = ROOT.parent\n",
    "if str(ROOT / \"src\") not in sys.path:\n",
    "    sys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT / \"src\"))\n",
    "print(\"Repository root:\", ROOT)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "673aeeb08cfb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. What is the engineering question?\n",
    "\n",
    "For a safety-critical system, asking whether it works is not enough. Start with four questions: **what can go wrong, how likely is it, what are the consequences, and how can the risk be reduced?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Working definitions:\n",
    "- **fault:** a cause of error or a faulty condition;\n",
    "- **error:** deviation from the required internal state;\n",
    "- **failure:** loss of a required function;\n",
    "- **hazard:** a potentially harmful condition;\n",
    "- **risk:** an engineering characterization of uncertain events and their consequences.\n",
    "\n",
    "A simple teaching approximation is $R=P(H)C$. This is an introductory model, not a universal definition.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "85f2ef987b76",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Reliability by hand\n",
    "\n",
    "Let $T$ be the time to failure. $R(t)=P(T>t)$ and $F(t)=1-R(t)$. With a constant failure rate:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$R(t)=e^{-\\lambda t},\\qquad F(t)=1-e^{-\\lambda t},\\qquad MTTF=1/\\lambda.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Calculate reliability at 1,000 hours for $\\lambda=2\\cdot10^{-4}\\,h^{-1}$.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "492ab00bbe71",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from math import exp\n",
    "failure_rate = 2e-4\n",
    "t = 1000\n",
    "R_t = exp(-failure_rate*t)\n",
    "F_t = 1-R_t\n",
    "print(f\"R({t} h)={R_t:.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"F({t} h)={F_t:.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"MTTF={1/failure_rate:.0f} h\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6669b8482999",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Interpreting the result\n",
    "\n",
    "The exponential model is **memoryless** and assumes constant $\\lambda$. Wear, ageing or periodic loading can invalidate this assumption. Check the modeling assumptions before applying the equation.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "62325dfc1fc6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "times=np.linspace(0,5000,200)\n",
    "for lam in [1e-4,2e-4,5e-4]:\n",
    "    plt.plot(times,np.exp(-lam*times),label=f\"lambda={lam:g} 1/h\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Time [h]\"); plt.ylabel(\"R(t)\"); plt.grid(alpha=.3); plt.legend(); plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3d5f0fd83860",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Repairable systems and availability\n",
    "\n",
    "For a two-state exponential failure-repair model, $A_\\infty=\\mu/(\\lambda+\\mu)$. Expressed using mean up- and down-times:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$A=\\frac{MTTF}{MTTF+MTTR}.$$\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "546201ca8103",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "MTTF=2000\n",
    "MTTR=8\n",
    "A=MTTF/(MTTF+MTTR)\n",
    "print(f\"Availability={A:.6f} = {A*100:.3f}%\")\n",
    "print(f\"Expected unavailability per year={(1-A)*8760:.1f} h\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b8eb241b0bdd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Series and parallel structures\n",
    "\n",
    "For independent components, $R_s=R_1R_2$. For two parallel components performing the same function, $R_p=1-(1-R_1)(1-R_2)$. The parallel formula only represents the redundancy correctly when failure dependencies have been addressed.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b37ae13e618c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "R1,R2=.98,.97\n",
    "pd.DataFrame({\"structure\":[\"series\",\"parallel\"],\"R\":[R1*R2,1-(1-R1)*(1-R2)]})\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3f4ffd26df27",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. TK-101: the initial hazard chain\n",
    "\n",
    "The common case is a flammable-liquid receiving and storage system: **TK-101 tank, LT-101 level transmitter, LSHH-101 protection, XV-101 shutoff valve, HMI/operator, CMMS/maintenance, containment and fire protection**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Initial narrative chain:\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sensor degradation -> deferred maintenance -> degraded protection -> high-level challenge -> failed shutdown/intervention -> overflow -> ignition -> fire.**\n",
    "\n",
    "This is an illustrative scenario, not a claim that every event must occur in exactly this order.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "ce356d2f943e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "p_challenge=.08\n",
    "p_auto_fail=.015\n",
    "p_manual_fail=.0592\n",
    "p_overflow=p_challenge*p_auto_fail*p_manual_fail\n",
    "print(f\"Approximate independent-event P(overflow)={p_overflow:.8f}\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fd18287e99e3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Try it!\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Increase the failure rate to 5e-4 and interpret R(1000).\n",
    "2. Calculate series and parallel reliability for two R=0.95 components.\n",
    "3. Give two reasons why the initial TK-101 independence assumptions could fail.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ed0120f61c40",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "Reliability concerns successful function delivery; availability also accounts for repair. Risk connects probability and consequence. The next unit structures **what can go wrong?** using PHA, FMEA and HAZOP.\n"
   ]
  }
